    <h2>What this page could not verify<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <div class="warn">
      Four things, stated plainly so you know which parts of this page are reads and which are reconstruction.
      <br><br>
      <b>1. What the four stain numbers actually compute.</b> The digits <b>5</b>, <b>7</b>, <b>8</b> and <b>8</b> are solid — two independent passes agreed on all four, and on the green centre holding a question mark. What the level never does is state the operation, and the recording never holds a frame where the centre is revealed. This page therefore prints no answer for the centre rather than guessing at one of several arithmetic rules.
      <br><br>
      <b>2. Which dial state is the target.</b> Both readings are reliable — <b>6 / 0 / 3 / 7</b> at 5:29 and <b>2 / 4 / 1 / 5</b> at 5:49 — but the footage between them is cut, so we cannot tell whether the second state was reached by solving something or was simply the next value in a cycle. Treat them as two observed states, not as a before-and-after solution.
      <br><br>
      <b>3. The exact switch pattern on the switchboard.</b> We can state the door formula (<b>blue × red + green</b>) and the six bar colours with confidence, because both were read from clean close-ups. What we cannot do is state which toggles end up on: the panel is only on screen in short bursts, the toggle row is small in frame, and two passes over the same second disagreed on whether there are five or six toggles. The bar count is six; treat the toggle count as unresolved.
      <br><br>
      <b>4. What the wine-rack colours are for.</b> The caps are visibly mismatched and there are twelve of them, but no frame in the recording links a specific cap colour to a specific bar on the switchboard or a specific quadrant on the dial. The connection is strongly implied by the room's design and is never demonstrated, so it stays a description rather than a step.
    </div>
